97 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 69 0 R
/E (Modern operating systems have become increasingly powerful, supporting a wide range of hardware, applications, and services. However, despite decades of advancement, the primary method of interacting with these systems still requires users to understand technical concepts, command-line interfaces, system utilities, configuration files, and complex error messages. When users encounter issues such as application crashes, boot failures, driver conflicts, permission errors, memory faults, or unexpected system behavior, they are often expected to diagnose the problem by manually inspecting logs, searching through documentation, reading online forums, or copying commands from external sources without fully understanding their purpose. This approach is inefficient, time-consuming, and frequently results in incorrect configurations or additional system instability.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
98 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 69 0 R
/E (The challenge is not limited to beginners. Even experienced users and software developers spend a significant amount of time identifying the root cause of system failures, interpreting diagnostic information, and determining the appropriate recovery process. Existing operating systems generally provide logs and error messages but offer limited assistance in helping users understand what those messages mean or what actions should be taken next. As systems continue to increase in complexity, the gap between the information presented by the operating system and the user's ability to interpret that information continues to grow.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
99 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 69 0 R
/E (Recent advances in artificial intelligence have demonstrated the ability of large language models to understand natural language, analyze technical information, summarize complex data, and provide contextual guidance. However, in most existing operating systems, these capabilities are introduced as optional productivity features rather than being integrated into the core interaction model of the system. As a result, users must still switch between the operating system, web browsers, documentation, search engines, and external AI tools to diagnose problems or complete routine system administration tasks.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
100 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 69 0 R
/E (There is an opportunity to redesign the interaction model of modern operating systems by incorporating artificial intelligence as a core system assistant while maintaining a secure separation between critical operating system components and AI services. Instead of requiring users to memorize commands, interpret cryptic diagnostic messages, or manually search for solutions, the operating system should be capable of understanding natural language requests, interpreting system state, analyzing structured diagnostic information, explaining failures in an understandable manner, recommending safe corrective actions, and assisting users with routine administrative tasks. Such an approach has the potential to reduce troubleshooting time, improve system accessibility, simplify day-to-day interaction, and create a more intuitive computing experience without compromising the stability, reliability, or security of the operating system itself.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
