/T (1.2 Why does this problem exist or matter? )
/E (1.2 Why does this problem exist or matter? )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
107 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 70 0 R
/E (The complexity of modern operating systems has grown significantly over the past several decades. Originally designed for technical users and system administrators, operating systems gradually evolved to support millions of applications, diverse hardware architectures, networking capabilities, virtualization, security mechanisms, cloud services, and countless background processes. While these advancements have made operating systems more powerful, they have also increased the complexity of managing and troubleshooting them.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
108 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 70 0 R
/E (Most operating systems continue to expose low-level technical details directly to users. Error messages often describe what failed rather than why it failed or how it can be resolved. Diagnostic information is typically distributed across log files, terminal outputs, event viewers, and system utilities, requiring users to manually collect, interpret, and correlate information before identifying the root cause of a problem. This process assumes that users possess technical knowledge that many do not have.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
109 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 70 0 R
/E (Traditional operating systems were also designed around command-based interaction rather than intent-based interaction. Users are expected to know the exact syntax of commands, configuration options, file locations, and system utilities before performing administrative tasks. Even simple objectives often require multiple commands and familiarity with operating system internals. Consequently, users frequently rely on internet searches, documentation, community forums, or trial-and-error approaches instead of receiving contextual assistance directly from the operating system.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
110 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 70 0 R
/E (Another reason this problem persists is the separation between operating systems and modern artificial intelligence technologies. Existing operating systems generally treat AI as an external productivity feature rather than an integrated system capability. As a result, users must manually switch between the operating system and external AI tools or search engines to diagnose problems, understand system behavior, or obtain guidance. This fragmented workflow interrupts productivity and increases the time required to resolve issues.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
111 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 70 0 R
/E (Finally, operating systems prioritize stability, performance, and security over user guidance. Their primary responsibility is to manage hardware resources and execute software reliably, not to explain internal behavior or educate users about system operations. While this design philosophy has been successful for decades, advances in artificial intelligence create an opportunity to rethink how users interact with operating systems. By enabling systems to understand natural language, interpret diagnostic information, and provide contextual assistance while preserving security boundaries, future operating systems can become significantly more intuitive, accessible, and efficient without compromising their fundamental responsibilities.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
